From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 22:43:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(control): traces must not block the thread they are
 traced from (KD-09)

GhosttyControlCore::_trace wrote straight to OutputDebugStringW on the
calling thread, three calls per line, under a comment claiming
"OutputDebugString costs nothing when no debugger is attached".

Measured on this machine, with a listener that had stopped draining the
machine-wide DBWIN channel: 10,005 ms per call. DBWIN's writers wait on
DBWIN_BUFFER_READY and give up only after a ten-second timeout, and the
shared events outliving whoever created them is enough to trigger it -
no debugger required. A pane traces about forty lines while it starts,
on the UI thread, at three calls each, so the terminal window took
minutes to appear. It was reported as "nothing pops up, the terminal is
never shown".

Traces now go through a bounded queue drained by one worker: a wedged
channel costs a trace and never the terminal. Formatting stays on the
caller so a line still reflects the moment it was traced, and it is one
call per line rather than three - which also makes each DBWIN record a
whole line, so the release gate's [ghostty] match reads better than it
did.

Verified with the channel deliberately still wedged and then clear:
traces arrive as single lines, engine identification still works, and a
launch reaches a visible window in 1.3 s. Suites unchanged: terminalCore
54/54, unitSettingsModel 159/159, unitControl 63/63, localTerminalApp
76/76.

Not covered: WT's own #ifdef _DEBUG traces write synchronously on the UI
thread too, so a Debug build still crawls on a wedged channel. Those are
upstream's and absent from a Release build; ours were the only
unconditional ones.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 90 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 85 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 30baf9038..dc111942e 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -9,6 +9,13 @@
 
 #include "GhosttyControlCore.g.cpp"
 
+// For the trace sink below - a queue and a worker, so a wedged debugger channel
+// cannot stall the UI thread.
+#include <condition_variable>
+#include <deque>
+#include <mutex>
+#include <thread>
+
 using namespace ::Microsoft::Terminal::Core;
 using namespace winrt::Windows::Foundation;
 using namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::TerminalConnection;
@@ -674,8 +681,83 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
     }
 
     // Sizing and DPI bugs here are invisible from the outside - the pane just
-    // looks wrong - and a packaged app has no stdout. OutputDebugString costs
-    // nothing when no debugger is attached and shows up in cdb when one is.
+    // looks wrong - and a packaged app has no stdout, so the traces go to the
+    // debugger channel where `scripts\smoke-release.ps1` can also read them and
+    // prove which engine a window is running.
+    //
+    // They go out on their own thread, and that is not tidiness.
+    // OutputDebugString is **not** the free call this code used to claim it was
+    // when no debugger is attached: DBWIN is a machine-wide channel, and once
+    // anything has attached to it and stopped servicing it - a debugger that was
+    // killed, a script that exited without closing the shared events - every
+    // writer on the machine waits on DBWIN_BUFFER_READY and gives up only after
+    // **ten seconds**, per call. Measured here at 10,005 ms a call after a probe
+    // script leaked its listener. A pane traces about forty lines while it comes
+    // up, on the UI thread, so the terminal window took minutes to appear and
+    // looked for all the world like a hang - reported as "nothing pops up, the
+    // terminal is never shown".
+    //
+    // A bounded queue drained by one worker means a wedged channel costs a
+    // trace and never the terminal. Formatting stays on the caller so the line
+    // reflects the moment it was traced.
+    namespace
+    {
+        class TraceSink
+        {
+        public:
+            static TraceSink& Get()
+            {
+                // Deliberately leaked. The worker has to outlive static
+                // destruction: a trace emitted while the process is tearing
+                // itself down must not reach a half-destroyed object.
+                static TraceSink* sink = new TraceSink();
+                return *sink;
+            }
+
+            void Write(std::wstring line)
+            {
+                {
+                    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> guard{ _mutex };
+                    // Drop rather than grow without bound or wait, which is the
+                    // whole point of being here.
+                    if (_queue.size() >= 512)
+                    {
+                        return;
+                    }
+                    _queue.push_back(std::move(line));
+                }
+                _cv.notify_one();
+            }
+
+        private:
+            TraceSink()
+            {
+                std::thread{ [this]() { _run(); } }.detach();
+            }
+
+            void _run()
+            {
+                for (;;)
+                {
+                    std::wstring line;
+                    {
+                        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> guard{ _mutex };
+                        _cv.wait(guard, [this]() { return !_queue.empty(); });
+                        line = std::move(_queue.front());
+                        _queue.pop_front();
+                    }
+                    // One call per line, not three: each is a round trip on the
+                    // channel, and three was three times the exposure.
+                    OutputDebugStringW(line.c_str());
+                }
+            }
+
+            std::mutex _mutex;
+            std::condition_variable _cv;
+            std::deque<std::wstring> _queue;
+        };
+    }
+
     void GhosttyControlCore::_trace(const wchar_t* format, ...)
     {
         wchar_t buf[512];
@@ -685,9 +767,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         va_end(args);
         if (n > 0)
         {
-            OutputDebugStringW(L"[ghostty] ");
-            OutputDebugStringW(buf);
-            OutputDebugStringW(L"\n");
+            TraceSink::Get().Write(L"[ghostty] " + std::wstring{ buf } + L"\n");
         }
     }
 
